questions.question:
Write a function countPairs(n) with:
 for i from 1 to n:
   for j from 1 to floor(n/i):
     // O(1) work
What is the overall time complexity and space usage? Provide the tight bound, discuss best/worst-case, and compare to a memory-efficient variant that avoids the inner loop entirely?

questions.explanation:
## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to derive nested-loop complexity where inner bound shrinks with i, revealing a harmonic-sum growth.

## Key Concepts
- Harmonic series: sum floor(n/i) ~ n log n
- Theta vs O, space O(1)
- Closed-form approximations vs exact sums

## Code Example
```javascript
function countPairs(n){
  let count=0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let j=1;j<=Math.floor(n/i);j++){
      count++;
    }
  }
  return count;
}
```

