## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to derive a streaming heavy-hitters approach with bounded memory and clear trade-offs. It focuses on a practical, beginner-friendly variant of the classic Space-Saving problem, tying implementation to batch processing and real-time constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Space-Saving / heavy-hitters
- Fixed-memory streaming
- Per-element vs per-batch costs

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode for Space-Saving with K counters
class Counter { constructor(id, count) { this.id = id; this.count = count; } }
let counters = new Map();
function update(e){
  if (counters.has(e)) { counters.get(e).count++ }
  else {
    if (counters.size < K) { counters.set(e, {count:1}) } 
    else { // replace min
      let minEntry = null; let minCount = Infinity;
      for (let [id, c] of counters) { if (c.count < minCount) { minCount = c.count; minEntry = id; } }
      counters.delete(minEntry);
      counters.set(e, {count: minCount + 1});
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adjust if the batch size B varies?
- How would you guarantee no false positives beyond a fixed error margin?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests dynamic skyline maintenance with batch updates, including amortized analysis and practical data-structure design.

## Key Concepts
- **2D dominance and Pareto frontier**: A point p dominates q if p.x ≥ q.x and p.y ≥ q.y with at least one strict inequality.
- **Invariant**: Frontier F is sorted by x with strictly increasing y, ensuring no two frontier points dominate each other.
- **Amortized analysis**: Each point is inserted once and removed at most once over n total points.
- **Space**: Uses O(n) space for all historical points plus O(|F|) for the current frontier.

## Data Structure
Use a balanced binary search tree (e.g., TreeMap) keyed by x-coordinate, with y-values stored alongside. This enables O(log |F|) insertion, O(1) access to the rightmost (largest x) point, and O(|F|) in-order traversal.

## Algorithm
1. Sort incoming batch by x-coordinate (O(B log B)).
2. Merge with frontier: for each batch point p, if p.y > current max frontier y, insert p and prune any frontier points with y ≤ p.y.
3. Enumerate frontier via in-order traversal.

## Code Example
```javascript
class Skyline {
  constructor() { this.frontier = new Map(); }

  insertBatch(points) {
    const sorted = points.sort((a, b) => a.x - b.x);
    let maxY = 0;

    for (const p of sorted) {
      if (p.y <= maxY) continue;

      for (const [x, y] of this.frontier) {
        if (y <= p.y) this.frontier.delete(x);
      }

      this.frontier.set(p.x, p.y);
      maxY = p.y;
    }
  }

  getFrontier() {
    return Array.from(this.frontier.entries())
      .sort((a, b) => a[0] - b[0]);
  }
}
```

## Complexity Summary
| Operation | Cost |
|-----------|------|
| Batch insertion | O(B log B + |F|) amortized |
| Enumerate frontier | O(|F|) |
| Space | O(n) |

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates your ability to design streaming top-k algorithms within a sliding window using compact probabilistic data structures and articulate clear trade-offs between accuracy, space, and time.

## Key Concepts

- **Sliding window processing**: Maintaining state only over the most recent W elements
- **Space-Saving sketch**: A fixed-memory algorithm that provides approximate frequency counts
- **Top-k aggregation**: Identifying the k most frequent items in a stream
- **Approximation guarantees**: Bounding error in frequency estimates
- **Time/space complexity**: Analyzing per-operation and batch performance

## Code Example

```javascript
class SlidingTopK {
  constructor(k, W) {
    this.k = k;
    this.W = W;
    this.counters = new Map(); // item -> count
    this.window = []; // queue of last W items
  }

  add(x) {
    if (this.counters.has(x)) {
      this.counters.set(x, this.counters.get(x) + 1);
    } else if (this.counters.size < this.k) {
      this.counters.set(x, 1);
    } else {
      const minEntry = this.findMinCounter();
      this.counters.delete(minEntry.key);
      this.counters.set(x, minEntry.value + 1);
    }
    this.window.push(x);
    if (this.window.length > this.W) {
      this.expire(this.window.shift());
    }
  }

  expire(x) {
    if (this.counters.has(x)) {
      const newCount = this.counters.get(x) - 1;
      if (newCount > 0) {
        this.counters.set(x, newCount);
      } else {
        this.counters.delete(x);
      }
    }
  }

  findMinCounter() {
    let minEntry = { key: null, value: Infinity };
    for (const [key, value] of this.counters) {
      if (value < minEntry.value) {
        minEntry = { key, value };
      }
    }
    return minEntry;
  }

  topK() {
    return [...this.counters.entries()]
      .sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1])
      .slice(0, this.k);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests streaming heavy hitters and fixed-memory reasoning. Misra-Gries demonstrates how to bound error while tracking potential mode candidates.

## Key Concepts
- Misra-Gries algorithm; heavy-hitters with fixed counters
- Online updates, O(1) amortized per element
- Memory O(k); approximate answer with additive error

## Code Example
